.TH "UBERTOOTH\-BTBR" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-btbr\fR \- experimental Classic Bluetooth connections and monitoring
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-btbr [\-U <index|serial>] [\-f <freq_offset>] [\-b <max_ac_errors>]
ubertooth\-btbr [\-U <index|serial>] \-V
ubertooth\-btbr \-h
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ubertooth\-btbr(1) takes part in Classic Bluetooth itself, rather than only listening: it can run an inquiry, answer one, page a device, be paged, and watch a connection being set up\. It opens an interactive prompt, \fBbt>\fR, and each command there starts one operation on the board; \fB?\fR lists them\.
.P
It is \fBexperimental\fR, and it needs the separate \fBbtbr\fR firmware image\. The other tools, including ubertooth\-rx(1) and ubertooth\-btle(1), need \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR, so a board is set up for one or the other and switching means reflashing it\. See FIRMWARE below\.
.P
At startup the tool gives the board the address \fB11:22:33:44:55:66\fR and the local name \fBUbertooth\fR, which is what an inquiring device sees in the Extended Inquiry Response (EIR)\. Change the address with the \fBbdaddr\fR command\.
.P
The host side is the Python module \fBubtbr\fR, which also implements enough of the Link Manager Protocol (LMP) to set up an unencrypted connection\.
.SH "FIRMWARE"
Flash the \fBbtbr\fR image, from a build of \fBfirmware/btbr\fR or from a release archive:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu \-d firmware/btbr/btbr\.dfu \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
and flash \fBbluetooth_rxtx\.dfu\fR again to go back to the other tools:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-dfu \-d firmware/bluetooth_rxtx/bluetooth_rxtx\.dfu \-r
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Add \fB\-U <index|serial>\fR to either command to pick one board of several; it works from both images\.
.P
Starting \fBubertooth\-btbr\fR on a board running \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR fails with \fBerror: could not set up Ubertooth <serial>: LIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT [\-7]\fR, followed by a \fBnote:\fR naming that firmware and the image to flash\. \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR offers the endpoint this tool writes to but never reads it, so the tool's first requests sit there until the write times out\. The serial in that line is the board the session got, which is worth comparing with the one \fBbtbr\fR was flashed onto\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
which Ubertooth to use\. See SELECTING A DEVICE\.
.TP
\fB\-f <0\-65535>\fR
frequency offset, written to the radio's \fBMOD_OFFSET\fR register\. Sent to the board before the prompt opens, and only when given; a run without it leaves whatever the firmware set\.
.TP
\fB\-b <0\-65535>\fR
how many bit errors a received access code may have and still count as a match\. A higher value finds more packets and more noise\.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version and end the command: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of the board \fB\-U\fR names, or of each attached board\. The same answer every other tool gives, including with no board attached; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\. This tool answers it by running ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-V\fR, so that tool has to be on the \fBPATH\fR, and is reported as \fBerror: could not run ubertooth\-util: No such file or directory\fR when it is not\. No \fBbtbr\fR firmware is needed for it: the version is read from whatever image the board is carrying\. It needs no Python \fBlibusb1\fR package either: the command line is read before the module that package ships is imported, so \fB\-V\fR answers on a host where it is not installed\. See DIAGNOSTICS\.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\. Like \fB\-V\fR, it needs no Python \fBlibusb1\fR package\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR both end the command, and both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both: the version first and the usage text last, whichever order they were written in\. The status is then \fB\-V\fR's, so a board that could not be read makes a command line carrying \fB\-h\fR exit 1, with the usage text printed all the same\.
.P
Each option may be given once, except \fB\-V\fR and \fB\-h\fR, which carry no value and say the same thing however often they are written\. See DIAGNOSTICS for what a command line it will not take does\.
.SH "SELECTING A DEVICE"
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR chooses one of several attached boards, as described in ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE: an index is a position in the list of attached Ubertooths (\fB\-U 0\fR is the first), and a serial\-number string selects that board whatever the enumeration order\. A serial may be shortened to its first few characters, in either case, and an all\-digits string is read as an index, so include a non\-digit (\fB\-U 05f\fR rather than \fB\-U 05\fR)\. If those characters start more than one attached board's serial, the tool lists the matching serials and selects neither; boards reporting the same serial are told apart by index instead\.
.P
An index names a board without reading any serial, so it disturbs none of the others\. Matching a serial has to read every attached board's, which means opening each one briefly; a board that does not answer is counted in the refusal, because the serial given may well be its own\.
.P
With more than one board attached and no \fB\-U\fR, the tool lists them and opens none:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: multiple Ubertooth devices found; select one with \-U <index|serial>
  Device 0: 1cc001038088cb956c43ad61c52000f5
  Device 1: 346001058088cb95345bad61c52000f5
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
It used to open whichever board libusb handed over first, which on such a host reached an arbitrary one: a board flashed with \fBbtbr\fR could be ignored in favour of one still carrying \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR, whose write timeout then looked like the \fBbtbr\fR board failing\.
.P
A board whose serial number cannot be read is listed as \fBserial unreadable\fR and can still be chosen by index\. Reading it means opening the board, so what makes it fail is an open that fails \(em on Linux, usually the udev rules not being installed \(em or a board that has left the bus\. A board whose interface another program has claimed reads its serial normally and fails later, when the session starts\.
.SH "PROMPT COMMANDS"
An address is written \fB11:22:33:44:55:66\fR or \fB112233445566\fR\.
.TP
\fBinquiry\fR
discover nearby devices; each response is reported with its EIR
.TP
\fBinquiry_scan\fR
answer inquiries, so nearby devices discover this board
.TP
\fBpage_scan\fR
accept a connection from a device that pages this board; a standard Classic controller usually gets through in a few seconds, while another Ubertooth paging this one is much less reliable (issue #807 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/807\fR)
.TP
\fBdiscoverable\fR
\fBinquiry_scan\fR and \fBpage_scan\fR together
.TP
\fBpage <BD_ADDR>\fR
connect to a device as the central, and keep the connection up
.TP
\fBl2ping <BD_ADDR>\fR
page a device and then exchange Logical Link Control and Adaptation Protocol (L2CAP) echo packets with it
.TP
\fBmonitor <BD_ADDR>\fR
watch for a connection being set up to the given peripheral; see MONITOR MODE
.TP
\fBbdaddr <BD_ADDR>\fR
set this board's address
.TP
\fBidle\fR
stop the running command and return the board to standby
.TP
\fBexit\fR, \fBq\fR, \fBx\fR, Ctrl\-D
leave the tool
.TP
\fB?\fR, \fB? <command>\fR
list the commands, or describe one
.P
A word that is none of these is refused, and the prompt stays up; see DIAGNOSTICS\.
.P
One command runs at a time\. Starting another while one is still running is refused with a \fBNot idle\fR line; \fBidle\fR or Ctrl\-C stops the running one first\.
.P
Ctrl\-C at the prompt stops the running command, as \fBidle\fR does, and leaves the prompt up; pressing it again while that command is stopping returns to the prompt\. Before the prompt is up, Ctrl\-C ends the tool\.
.SH "MONITOR MODE"
\fBmonitor\fR targets the paging phase of a connection, where the central sends the peripheral a Frequency Hop Synchronization (FHS) packet carrying its full address and clock\. If the Ubertooth receives that FHS before the real peripheral does, it joins the new piconet ahead of both devices and reports the packets they exchange, including the unencrypted LMP traffic that opens every connection\. An LMP packet that sets the Adaptive Frequency Hopping (AFH) map is followed, so the capture keeps up when the piconet narrows its channels\.
.P
Give it the address of the peripheral being connected to, then make the connection from the other device:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btbr
bt> monitor 11:22:33:44:55:66
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
If no FHS appears, the peripheral won the race; try again\.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
.IP "\(bu" 4
The CC2400 radio demodulates Basic Rate only, so Enhanced Data Rate packets cannot be received\. The packet types handled are ID, NULL, POLL, FHS, DM and DH\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Encryption is not implemented\. Packets sent after a connection turns encryption on are reported as raw data, and the AFH map stops being followed, because the LMP packet carrying it is encrypted too\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
The link layer carries Asynchronous Connection\-Less (ACL) traffic only, and does not do role switching or the LMP procedures around it\. A peer asking to send multi\-slot packets is refused as well, and the feature mask this end sends does not offer them, so a connection stays on single\-slot ones: the firmware decodes the multi\-slot kinds, but no capture has exercised that over the air\. A request for any other procedure there is no handler for is refused too, rather than left unanswered, so the peer can get on with the link instead of waiting out a 30\-second LMP response timeout; a PDU that expects no answer is ignored\. Either way the opcode is named in a \fBwarning:\fR line, once per opcode for the life of the connection\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A peer may refuse any of the five things a connection opens by asking it for: its features, extended features, version, name, and encryption key size mask\. Each refusal is reported with the reason the peer gave, and the next request goes out as it would after an answer\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A PDU too short to carry an opcode is reported in a \fBwarning:\fR line and the connection goes on\. There is nothing to answer in one: an escape opcode says the opcode is in the PDU's second byte, and a PDU of one byte has none\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
A PDU that does carry its opcode but ends before its parameters is answered instead: the name request, the extended features request, and the AFH set are refused with invalid LMP parameters, and a short slot offset is ignored, since that PDU expects no answer\. \fBmonitor\fR has no peer to answer, so it reports such a PDU and leaves the AFH map as it was\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
As the central, it polls the peripheral hard enough to drain its battery\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBmonitor\fR is a race against the real peripheral and often takes several attempts\. Packets are lost: get closer, use a better antenna\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "OUTPUT"
The packets a command decodes go to standard output, one to a line, each carrying the time it was printed:
.IP "" 4
.nf
19:18:58 RX FHS: FHS: bdaddr=112233445566 parity=6c38a7bd cls=5a020c ltaddr=0 clk=365c
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That time is the only timing a session has\. An inquiry response is the response itself and the EIR fields under it, each as a timed line of its own:
.IP "" 4
.nf
19:19:03 ACL(llid=L2CAP_START, flow=1, len=11): 0a 09 55 62 65 72 74 6f 6f 74 68
19:19:03 COMPLETE_LOCAL_NAME: 'Ubertooth'
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A session holding a connection \-\- \fBpage\fR, \fBl2ping\fR, \fBpage_scan\fR, \fBdiscoverable\fR \-\- reports each LMP packet the same way, with the baseband header and the PDU named and in hex\. It also traces the same exchange on standard error, where the link manager adds the state it was in and the seconds since the connection came up; that trace is a diagnostic, and the capture is what standard output holds\.
.P
A connection the peer hangs up ends with the reason the peer gave, on standard error with the rest of the link manager's trace:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Peer detached: reason 0x13, the peer's user ended the connection
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The prompt, the \fBType ? to list commands\fR line and what \fB?\fR and \fB? <command>\fR print are on standard error, with the progress a command makes, as plain lines carrying neither the time nor a level\. The prompt has no newline of its own, so the first progress line after it lands on its end, as it does on a terminal:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Type ? to list commands
bt> Starting BTCtlInquiryCmd
Inquiry state Ready
Inquiry done
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Every line of a capture can therefore be read by the time in front of it, whatever the user types while it runs: the prompt carries no newline of its own, and on standard output it would land on the front of the next packet, while \fB?\fR is the tool describing itself rather than capture data\. The usage text \fB\-h\fR prints is on standard output, which is the whole of what that run was asked for\.
.P
A session at a terminal leaves the prompt to the terminal's line editor, which draws it on the terminal as it always has and keeps the cursor right while a line is edited\. Nothing is reading standard output there, so nothing is in the way\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries the packets a command decodes and the usage text \fB\-h\fR prints; see OUTPUT\. Everything else, the intro line, what \fB?\fR prints and the prompt of a session nobody is editing at a terminal included, goes to standard error\. A command line the tool will not take, a board it cannot select, open or set up, and anything that goes wrong during a session, are reported as the one\-line \fBerror:\fR, \fBwarning:\fR and \fBnote:\fR lines the other tools use\.
.P
A host without the Python \fBlibusb1\fR package can still ask what is installed: \fB\-V\fR, \fB\-h\fR and a command line the tool will not take are all answered before the \fBusb1\fR module that package ships is imported\. A call that opens a board does need it, and says so in one line rather than a traceback:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not start the session: No module named 'usb1'
note: a session needs the Python libusb1 package, which ships the usb1 module; install it with "pip install libusb1"
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
An option value outside 0\-65535, or one that is not a number, is refused before the board is opened: \fBerror: \-f must be 0\-65535 (got bad)\fR, exit status 1\. An address the prompt will not take is one line, \fBerror: BD_ADDR must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got <word>)\fR, and leaves the prompt up; a \fB? <command>\fR naming no command is \fBerror: no help on '<word>'\fR, and so does\. A word that is no command at all is \fBerror: no such command '<word>'\fR, naming the first word of the line, and leaves the prompt up as well\. None of the three changes the session's exit status\.
.P
Every other command line the tool will not take is one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error with no usage text, and exit 1: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR, \fBerror: option '\-f' requires a value\fR, \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, and \fBerror: option '\-U' given more than once\fR for an option given twice, the line every tool uses (see ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES)\.
.P
During a session, a board that stops answering is reported and the running command ends rather than waiting\. A board that left the bus mid\-command gives
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: the receive thread stopped: USBErrorPipe: LIBUSB_ERROR_PIPE [\-9]
warning: BTCtlInquiryCmd: could not ask the device to go idle: LIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE [\-4]
warning: BTCtlInquiryCmd: the device did not go idle, stopping anyway
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
and a request that failed some other way gives \fBerror: <command> stopped: <type>: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR\. The prompt stays up in every case, and a command that will not stop at all leaves the board claimed, which is reported on the way out (see EXIT STATUS)\.
.P
Standard output going away is one line, \fBerror: standard output has gone away\fR, said once however many writes find it gone\. A capture piped into something that stops reading, as \fBubertooth\-btbr | head \-5\fR does, therefore leaves the prompt up with nothing more printed, and the session ends with exit 1; so does a \fB\-h\fR or \fB\-V\fR whose answer could not be delivered, whether its reader left or there was no standard output to print to at all, as \fBubertooth\-btbr \-h >&\-\fR leaves\. A session asked for with no standard output, or with one that was not opened to be written, as \fBubertooth\-btbr 1<&0\fR leaves, is refused in that same line, before any board is opened: the stream carries the capture, and the prompt cannot be read without it either\. A session that had printed nothing when the reader left has lost nothing and ends as it otherwise would\. If standard error has gone the same way, the exit status is all that is left to say it with\.
.P
Standard error going away costs the tool its diagnostics and nothing else\. One open for reading only (\fBubertooth\-btbr \-\-bogus 2<&0\fR), closed (\fBubertooth\-btbr \-\-bogus 2>&\-\fR), or a pipe whose reader has left (\fBubertooth\-btbr \-\-bogus 2>&1 | true\fR) all go the same way: the lines are dropped and the exit status is the command's own\. A session keeps its capture through it, wherever standard output is going: a warning in the middle of one used to end it and take the rest of the capture with it, and a refused command line used to exit 120 rather than 1, from the flush the interpreter makes as it exits\. A session with that stream closed used to be refused before the prompt was read\. A child the tool starts for \fB\-V\fR is handed the bin for that stream rather than a closed descriptor, whose number the first file it opened would take\.
.P
With no Ubertooth attached the tool says \fBerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR and exits 1\. A selection that names no board, or more than one, is refused the same way; see SELECTING A DEVICE\. A board that cannot be opened is \fBerror: could not open Ubertooth <serial>: <reason>\fR, with a \fBnote:\fR about file permissions when the open was refused for want of them\. A board another program is using opens, and is then refused when the session claims its USB interface: \fBerror: Ubertooth <serial> is in use by another program (libusb \-6)\fR, the line every tool gives for that\. Which libusb code it carries depends on the system, \fB\-6\fR on Linux and \fB\-3\fR on macOS; see ubertooth(7), WHEN A BOARD CANNOT BE USED\. A board that takes none of the requests the session opens with gets \fBerror: could not set up Ubertooth <serial>: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR and, when the request timed out, a \fBnote:\fR naming \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR and the image to flash: that is nearly always what such a board is carrying, though a board already carrying \fBbtbr\fR that stopped answering times out the same way\. See FIRMWARE\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 for \fB\-h\fR, and when the session ended cleanly, which is \fBexit\fR, \fBq\fR, \fBx\fR or Ctrl\-D with nothing left running and everything asked for printed\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take, when standard output went away before all that was asked for had been printed, when a session was asked for and the Python \fBlibusb1\fR package is not installed, when no Ubertooth is found, when \fB\-U\fR names no board or more than one, when the one it names cannot be opened or is in use by another program, when the board refuses the requests that set the session up, when Ctrl\-C ends the tool before the prompt is up, when the session ends with a command that would not stop still holding the board, and when the thread reading the board stopped on a failure\.
.P
The last of those is a board that left the USB bus, stopped answering, or sent something this tool could not read, with the session still up: the thread reading it ends there, nothing restarts it, and no message arrives for the rest of the session\. The board was power\-cycled under a running \fBinquiry\fR when this was found, and the session reported every step correctly and then exited 0, so a script could not tell it from a run where every command worked\. What ended that thread is said as it happens, \fBerror: the receive thread stopped: <type>: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR, and again on the way out as a \fBwarning:\fR\.
.P
A single command the board refused is not that\. It is reported on standard error (see DIAGNOSTICS), the prompt stays up, and the session goes on and ends with the status its own ending gives it, the way a shell reports a failed command without failing itself\.
